<tr><td>Q4</td><td>The staff roster, with no My Clay Hub box in Tinker HQ yet.</td><td><strong>Build the machinery now, no Tinker HQ UI.</strong> It mirrors managers/admins until Phase F adds the box. Strict input, fails closed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Q5</td><td>"Change email" isn't built; a delete + re-create gives a new memberId.</td><td><strong>Treat it as a new identity:</strong> the old memberId becomes a tombstone (<code>removed</code>), the new one starts fresh. B4 stays its own plan.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Q6 <em>(new)</em></td><td>A "Quick Log" pause request (no <code>scheduledAt</code>) is never started by Membership Manager, but <code>deriveStatus</code> treats its dates as a real pause, so My Clay Hub would show the member as <strong>paused</strong> (no booking) for those dates.</td><td><strong>Ask you.</strong> Option A: count it (dates are dates; staff fix the record). Option B: ignore pause records without <code>scheduledAt</code> until processed — a change to <code>deriveStatus</code>, reviewed on its own.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Q7 <em>(new)</em></td><td>When a member is removed, does My Clay Hub keep their name, email and phone digits?</td><td><strong>No: a tombstone keeps only</strong> <code>memberId</code>, <code>tombstone:true</code>, <code>status:'removed'</code>, <code>sourceReadTime</code>, <code>updatedAt</code>. Their profile is replaced the same way. (Membership Manager still has the record.)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Q8 <em>(new)</em></td><td>The safety stops at 66 members: "10% of statuses" is 7 people, and the 1st of a month can legitimately move that many (pauses starting and ending).</td><td><strong>Stop only above max(10%, 10 members)</strong> for status changes and max(5%, 4 members) for removals; the first fill (an empty collection) is exempt; a stop writes nothing and emails you.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="contract">The link contract (E-1 writes it down once; both sides test against it)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Part</th><th>Exact rule</th></tr>
<tr><td>Envelope</td><td><code>{v:1, kind, readTime, …}</code>. <code>kind</code> ∈ <code>member</code> | <code>staff</code> | <code>reconcile</code>. Any other key, version or kind → 400.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>readTime</code></td><td><code>{seconds: "&lt;decimal string&gt;", nanos: &lt;int 0..999999999&gt;}</code>, taken from the source read's <code>Timestamp</code> with nanosecond precision; stored as a Firestore <code>Timestamp</code>; compared as (seconds, nanos). Later → apply; equal or earlier → "stale" (200, nothing written). Out-of-range or non-canonical → 400.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>member</code></td><td><code>{memberId, snapshot}</code> or <code>{memberId, tombstone:true}</code>. <code>snapshot</code> is <strong>rebuilt</strong> from allowed keys, never copied and trimmed: <code>name</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>emailLower</code>, <code>stage</code>, <code>memberSince</code>, <code>phoneLast4</code>, <code>retired</code>, <code>scheduledPause: {startDate, endDate} | null</code>, <code>pauseHistory: [{startDate, endDate, priorTerm?}]</code> (legacy <code>start</code>/<code>end</code> mapped to the modern names, the modern value winning as in <code>deriveStatus</code>), <code>scheduledCancellation: {finalAccessDate} | null</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Malformed values</td><td>Never rejected, never carried raw: a string value goes as-is (<code>deriveStatus</code> judges it); a non-string where a date or stage belongs, or a non-object pause, becomes the marker string <code>"!malformed"</code>, which <code>deriveStatus</code> already treats as malformed → <code>review</code>. Tested for each.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>phoneLast4</code></td><td>The last 4 ASCII digits of <code>phone</code> after removing every non-digit; fewer than 4 digits or no phone → <code>null</code>. Computed in <code>tinker-hq-apps</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Computed on receipt</td><td><code>firstName</code> and <code>lastInitial</code> from <code>name</code>; <code>status</code> from <code>deriveStatus</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>staff</code></td><td><code>{uid, staff: {name, role, active, grant}}</code> or <code>{uid, tombstone:true}</code>. Sender rule, fail-closed: <code>role</code> must be exactly <code>admin</code>|<code>manager</code>|<code>staff</code>; <code>active</code> a boolean or absent (true); <code>appAccess</code> absent or an array of strings. <code>grant</code> = active and (role is manager/admin, or <code>appAccess</code> contains <code>my-clay-hub</code>). Anything malformed → tombstone + an alert naming the uid only. Never email, PIN or <code>appAccess</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>reconcile</code></td><td><code>{scope: 'members'|'staff', items: [the same member/staff entries for every live source record]}</code> — the complete set at one readTime. The receiver computes everything (see E-2).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tombstone (stored)</td><td>Replaces the document: <code>{memberId, tombstone:true, status:'removed', sourceReadTime, updatedAt}</code>; <code>memberProfiles/{memberId}</code> and <code>staffRoster/staff_{uid}</code> likewise. A later live snapshot restores only the allowlist.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Responses</td><td>200 <code>{result: applied|stale|unchanged}</code>; 400 for a malformed envelope; 409 for a reconcile stopped by a threshold; 5xx only for real failures. The sender throws (so retries happen) only on 5xx, 429 and timeouts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Logging</td><td>Never request bodies, names, emails, phone digits, notes or source documents — only memberId/uid, kind, result and counts.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="settings">Functions and their settings</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Function</th><th>Project / codebase</th><th>Trigger</th><th>Runs as</th><th>Settings</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>ingestMemberUpdate</code></td><td>my-clay-hub / <code>members</code></td><td>https, invoker <code>clayhub-link@tinker-hq-apps</code></td><td><code>ingest@</code></td><td>timeout 120 s (a reconcile is one call of at most 500 writes), memory 512MiB, cpu 1, concurrency 10, maxInstances 3</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>recomputeStatuses</code></td><td>my-clay-hub / <code>members</code></td><td>schedule <code>every day 09:30</code> UTC; retryCount 2, maxRetrySeconds 0, minBackoff 600, maxBackoff 1200, maxDoublings 1</td><td><code>ingest@</code></td><td>timeout 300 s, memory 256MiB, maxInstances 1, concurrency 1</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>onClayHubMemberWritten</code></td><td>tinker-hq-apps / <code>clayhub-link</code></td><td>Firestore <code>document.written</code> on <code>clayHub_members/{docId}</code>, <code>(default)</code>, nam5, retry true</td><td><code>clayhub-link@</code></td><td>timeout 60 s, memory 256MiB, maxInstances 5</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>onStaffUserWritten</code></td><td>tinker-hq-apps / <code>clayhub-link</code></td><td>Firestore <code>document.written</code> on <code>users/{uid}</code>, retry true</td><td><code>clayhub-link@</code></td><td>as above</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>reconcileLink</code></td><td>tinker-hq-apps / <code>clayhub-link</code></td><td>schedule <code>every 6 hours from 01:15 to 19:15</code> UTC (01:15, 07:15, 13:15, 19:15); retryCount 1, maxRetrySeconds 0, minBackoff 600, maxBackoff 600, maxDoublings 0</td><td><code>clayhub-link@</code></td><td>timeout 300 s, memory 512MiB, maxInstances 1, concurrency 1</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="note">Exact values are reviewed in E-2/E-4's PRs; the guard refuses anything that differs from the declaration. The ingest URL is a hard-coded constant in the sender, tested.</p>

<h2>Phases</h2>
<div class="note"><strong>Order:</strong> E-pre first. E-0, E-1, E-2 and E-3 can then proceed on separate branches. E-4 needs E-1 and E-3. E-5 before E-6. <strong>E-7 onward waits for every gate</strong> (listed in E-7). Every PR: Codex + Claude implementation review, then Christie's "okay to merge". Every deploy: that project's guard, <code>--diff</code> pasted, and Christie's "approved to change firebase &lt;sha&gt;" for that exact sha and project.</div>

<div class="phase">
<h3>E-pre — Decisions written down before any code <span class="status-tag not-ready">execution-ready: false</span></h3>
<p>Christie's answers to Q1–Q8 → DECISIONS rows in my-clay-hub; SPEC.md (§3, the recompute time) and DATA-MODEL.md (the contract above, the never-copy list, tombstones) updated in one docs PR.</p>
</div>

<div class="phase">
<h3>E-0 — studio-hub: the users-rules pins <span class="status-tag not-ready">execution-ready: false</span></h3>
<p>A comment on the <code>users</code> rules naming My Clay Hub's dependency (<code>name</code>, <code>role</code>, <code>active</code>, <code>appAccess</code>). Tests only for what isn't covered yet (rules.test.js already pins self-granted appAccess and studios): role self-promotion on create (<code>manager</code>, <code>admin</code>), and <code>active</code> on self-update. The comment ships through studio-hub's rules guard (free, sha phrase) so the deployed file matches <code>main</code>.</p>
<div class="bdd">Given a signed-in user with no users doc
When they create their own doc with role 'manager' (or 'admin')
Then the write is denied

Given a staff user
When they update their own doc changing active
Then the write is denied</div>
</div>

<div class="phase">
<h3>E-1 — The contract as code fixtures (both repos) <span class="status-tag not-ready">execution-ready: false</span></h3>
<p>One fixture set — source docs (including every forbidden field at every nesting level, both pause spellings, <code>priorTerm</code>, malformed dates/stages/pauses, phones of every shape, users docs with malformed role/active/appAccess) and the exact envelopes they must produce — committed byte-identical in both repos, with a test in each pinning its sha-256.</p>
<p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> both repos' tests load the same fixtures and fail if either copy changes.</p>
</div>

<div class="phase">
<h3>E-2 — my-clay-hub: the receiving side (codebase <code>members</code>) <span class="status-tag not-ready">execution-ready: false</span></h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong><code>ingestMemberUpdate</code></strong> (settings above). <code>member</code>/<code>staff</code>: one transaction — read the target doc(s), apply the readTime gate, derive status from the stored source shape, write <code>members</code> and <code>memberProfiles</code> together (or <code>staffRoster</code>), or replace them with tombstones.</li>
  <li><strong><code>reconcile</code></strong> (one call): validate the whole batch first; read every target; work out the effective change set after the readTime gate (additions, updates, status changes, removals = held live records absent from the batch); apply Q8's thresholds (denominator = live records held; additions don't count; the first fill into an empty collection is exempt); if it passes, write everything in one transaction (≤ 500 writes; refuse above that with an alert — far beyond today's size); if not, write nothing, answer 409, log the counts. Staff the same.</li>
  <li><strong><code>recomputeStatuses</code></strong> (09:30 UTC): a dry run over every live member first (Q8 threshold → stop, nothing written); then per member a transaction that re-reads the doc and re-derives from its current stored source shape, writing <code>members.status</code> and <code>memberProfiles.status</code> together only if it changed. An ingest landing in between is never overwritten, because the transaction re-reads.</li>
  <li><strong><code>firestore.rules</code></strong>: explicit deny-all blocks for <code>members</code>, <code>memberProfiles</code>, <code>staffRoster</code> with rules tests (no sign-in exists until Phase F, which adds a member's own-profile read). These deploy through the rules guard <em>before</em> the function.</li>
  <li>Declarations, iam-expectations (<code>ingest@</code> and its custom role), and a FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK section including the stop/resume runbook (below).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Tests first</strong> (emulator, Node 22): every fixture; the readTime gate (earlier, equal, same millisecond with different nanos, malformed); tombstone replaces all former personal data, re-appearance restores only the allowlist; every D4 row through the real <code>deriveStatus</code>, including <code>priorTerm</code> and each malformed value → <code>review</code>; reconcile: first fill exempt, exact threshold boundaries, small denominators, additions, already-tombstoned members, a stale item inside a batch, a stop leaving both collections untouched; recompute vs ingest in both orders, threshold stop with zero writes, tombstones excluded, profile kept in step; staff: unticking and demotion tombstone, a malformed role never grants; rules deny every client operation; no log line contains a fixture's name, email or phone.</p>
<div class="bdd">Given members/m_7Q holds sourceReadTime (100 s, 500 ns)
When a snapshot with readTime (100 s, 400 ns) arrives
Then nothing is written and the answer is "stale"

Given a reconcile batch that would remove 6 of 66 members
When it is posted
Then nothing is written, the answer is 409, and the stop alert fires

Given a source pause whose endDate is the number 20270201 (malformed)
When its snapshot is ingested
Then the member's status is 'review', and the stored pause holds "!malformed", not the number</div>
</div>

